{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 15, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from the year 1152 until his death in 1187 and he played a pivotal role within the Kingdom of Jerusalem during a time of great turmoil. His life story involves complex political maneuvering alongside military leadership that shaped the fate of the Crusader states in the Levant. He ascended to power after his father Raymond II suffered assassination by the Hashashin order so young Raymond inherited both title and responsibility at an early age.\n\n*Early Life and Succession Challenges*\nThe young count faced immediate threats from external enemies while also dealing with internal strife among the nobility of his realm. He spent several years as a prisoner of Nur ad-Din following a disastrous battle near Inab where his forces were defeated and captured. This period of captivity lasted roughly eight years and it profoundly influenced his worldview regarding Muslim powers and diplomatic relations. Upon his release he returned to find his county weakened yet he managed to restore stability through careful alliances and strategic marriages that strengthened his position against hostile neighbors.\n\n*Regency and Political Intrigue*\nRaymond later became regent for the leper king Baldwin IV because the monarch lacked an heir and suffered from debilitating illness. This role placed him at the center of fierce factional disputes between the court party led by Guy of Lusignan and the noble barons who supported Raymond. He opposed the marriage of Sibylla to Guy since he believed Guy was incompetent and dangerous for the survival of the kingdom. His opposition created deep rifts within the Christian leadership and these divisions ultimately hampered their ability to present a united front against Saladin who was gathering strength in Egypt and Syria.\n\n*Final Years and Legacy*\nThe conflict reached its peak during the events leading up to the Battle of Hattin where Raymond advocated for caution rather than engaging the enemy in open desert warfare. His advice was ignored by King Guy who marched the army into a trap resulting in catastrophic losses for the Crusader forces. Raymond escaped the slaughter but died shortly thereafter possibly from shame or illness while witnessing the collapse of the kingdom he tried to save. His legacy remains that of a pragmatic leader who understood the limitations of Crusader power yet could not prevent disaster due to the arrogance of rivals. The fall of Jerusalem followed swiftly after his death and marked the end of an era for the Latin East despite his best efforts to maintain peace and order through diplomacy instead of reckless war.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -1.6331805e-05}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.0002307562}, {"token": " as", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -3.5762793e-07}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.027738888}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -2.0955598}, {"token": " year", "logprob": -8.3446537e-07}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.3113031e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.218656e-06}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -9.536748e-07}, {"token": " until", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -1.2278632e-05}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -2.6226078e-06}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -5.960466e-07}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -3.9339143e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.549722e-06}, {"token": "8", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.0005452331}, {"token": " he", "logprob": -0.00060445454}, {"token": " played", "logprob": -0.04455208}, {"token": "